DEEP[1]=('Chapter 8 is named Akṣara-Brahma Yoga, the yoga of the imperishable Brahman. It is the Gītā’s chapter on death and what lies beyond.',
 'Commentators note that Arjuna calls Kṛṣṇa <i>Puruṣottama</i>, “Supreme Person”, a title explained in 15.18. He has been listening closely; every term from 7.29–30 is picked up. Śaṅkara says Arjuna wants to know the meaning of each, so that he can meditate rightly. Rāmānuja says each term names something to be known by those who seek different goals. The terms come from Upaniṣadic and Brāhmaṇa vocabulary of the time.',
 'When a teacher uses words you don’t understand, ask about each one. Clear words lead to clear thinking.')
DEEP[2]=('',
 'The seventh question is the most personal: how is the Lord known at the moment of death? Commentators note that the dying mind is weak and confused, so the question is practical. Śaṅkara says the question is how the self-controlled can fix their minds on the Lord when the senses fail. Rāmānuja says Arjuna asks how each type of seeker should meditate at the end. The Kaṭha Upaniṣad is built around a similar question, Naciketas asking Death what happens after death. The Gītā’s answer, in 8.5–14, is remembrance practised through life.',
 'Thinking about the end clarifies the present. What would you want to have in mind at your last moment?')
DEEP[3]=('',
 '<i>Akṣara</i> means both “imperishable” and “syllable”; the word links Brahman to Oṃ (8.13). The Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad (3.8.8–9) describes the Akṣara: not coarse, not fine, not short, not long; by its command the sun and moon stand apart. Commentators differ on <i>adhyātma</i>. Śaṅkara: Brahman dwelling in each body as the inner Self. Rāmānuja: the nature of the individual self, or the subtle body. <i>Visarga</i>, the creative “pouring forth”, is read as the cosmic act of creation, or as the offering in sacrifice that sustains beings (3.14). Brief definitions, but each opens a field.',
 'Big ideas become usable when defined simply. Write down in one line what you mean by your own key words.')
DEEP[4]=('',
 'Commentators explain the three terms. <i>Adhibhūta</i>: all that changes and perishes, the material world. <i>Adhidaiva</i>: the cosmic Person, <i>Puruṣa</i>, often identified with Hiraṇyagarbha, the cosmic mind from which the gods arise. <i>Adhiyajña</i>: the Lord himself, who receives all sacrifices and dwells in the body as inner witness. Śaṅkara says the Lord as adhiyajña is Viṣṇu, the deity of sacrifice. Rāmānuja says the Lord is the inner controller who receives every offering. The phrase <i>atra dehe</i>, “here in the body”, brings the Divine very close.',
 'The Divine, in this view, is not far off. It dwells within this very body, receiving every sincere offering.')
DEEP[5]=('',
 'This verse lies behind a widespread Hindu custom: chanting God’s name, or reading the Gītā, at the bedside of the dying. Tulasī leaves and Gaṅgā water are often given. Commentators stress that remembrance at the end cannot usually be forced; it grows from a life of remembrance. Śaṅkara says such a person reaches the Lord’s nature. Rāmānuja says he reaches the Lord himself. The story of Ajāmila in the Bhāgavata Purāṇa tells of a sinner who, dying, called his son Nārāyaṇa and was saved by the very name. Devotees love the story; teachers note it as grace, not a technique.',
 'Practise remembering what you love most, every day. That is the best preparation for any ending.')
DEEP[6]=('',
 'The story of Bharata (Jaḍa Bharata) is told in the Bhāgavata Purāṇa (Book 5). A king turned hermit, he became attached to an orphaned deer, thought of it at death, and was reborn as a deer. In his next life, wise from that lesson, he lived as a silent sage. Commentators use the story to explain <i>sadā tad-bhāva-bhāvita</i>, “always coloured by that thought”. The last thought is not random; it is the summary of a lifetime’s habits. Śaṅkara and Rāmānuja agree that the dying mind follows its strongest impressions. The verse is a strong argument for daily practice.',
 'What you think about most is quietly shaping you. Choose your daily thoughts as carefully as your food.')
DEEP[7]=('',
 '<i>Mām anusmara yudhya ca</i>, “remember me and fight”, is one of the Gītā’s shortest summaries. Commentators note the two verbs side by side: remembrance and action, not one instead of the other. Śaṅkara says constant remembrance purifies the mind, and the duty of fighting is done in that spirit. Rāmānuja says remembrance of the Lord while doing one’s duty is the essence of bhakti yoga. Gandhi often quoted this verse. For Arjuna it answers chapter 1: he can act and still be devoted, still be good.',
 'Do your work with the sacred in your heart. Remembrance and action belong together.')
DEEP[8]=('',
 '<i>Abhyāsa</i>, practice, links back to 6.35. Commentators note the method: repeated practice makes the mind <i>ananya-gāmin</i>, “not going to another”. Śaṅkara says practice means repeatedly bringing the mind to one object. Rāmānuja says it means constant meditation on the Lord. The goal, <i>paramaṃ puruṣaṃ divyam</i>, the supreme divine Person, is described in the next verse. The Gītā’s meditation is not empty; it has an object, the Lord, and a means, practice.',
 'A few minutes of daily practice, kept up for years, builds a mind that remembers on its own.')
DEEP[9]=('',
 'Eight descriptions in one verse, each a meditation. <i>Kavi</i>, seer or poet, the all-knowing. <i>Purāṇa</i>, ancient, without beginning. <i>Anuśāsitṛ</i>, ruler, governor of all. <i>Aṇor aṇīyān</i>, subtler than an atom; the Kaṭha Upaniṣad (1.2.20) adds “greater than the great”. <i>Dhātṛ</i>, upholder. <i>Acintya-rūpa</i>, of unthinkable form. <i>Āditya-varṇa</i>, sun-coloured, and <i>tamasaḥ parastāt</i>, beyond darkness, come from the Śvetāśvatara Upaniṣad (3.8): “I know that great Person, sun-coloured, beyond darkness”. The verse is in triṣṭubh metre.',
 'Pick one phrase, like “beyond darkness”, and hold it quietly for a few breaths. Let it light the mind.')
DEEP[10]=('',
 'The point between the eyebrows, <i>bhrū-madhya</i>, is the <i>ājñā cakra</i> in later yoga traditions, a centre of concentration. Commentators describe a yogic practice: gathering the life-breath upward through the subtle channel to the brow and then out through the crown. Śaṅkara explains it as a method for advanced yogis. Rāmānuja notes that devotion, <i>bhakti</i>, is named alongside yoga; technique without love is incomplete. Many teachers say ordinary people need not learn the technique. A steady, loving mind is its essence.',
 'You may never practise special techniques. A steady, loving mind is the heart of all of them.')
DEEP[11]=('',
 'This verse closely echoes the Kaṭha Upaniṣad (1.2.15): <i>sarve vedā yat padam āmananti… yad icchanto brahmacaryaṃ caranti, tat te padaṃ saṅgraheṇa bravīmy oṃ ity etat</i>, “the word which all the Vedas declare… desiring which people live as students, that word I tell you briefly: it is Oṃ”. Commentators note that the Gītā is following the Upaniṣad closely, and 8.13 completes the parallel with Oṃ. Three kinds of seeker are named: scholars, renunciants, and celibate students. All seek the same Imperishable.',
 'Different disciplines point to the same goal. Your practice may look different from another’s and still aim at the same light.')
DEEP[12]=('',
 'The “gates” are the senses, as in 5.13’s city of nine gates. Commentators describe three steps: close the gates (withdraw the senses); confine the mind in the heart (gather attention inward); raise the breath to the head (the yogic exit). Śaṅkara says the mind is fixed in the heart-lotus, the seat of the Self in Upaniṣadic thought. Rāmānuja agrees that the heart is the place of meditation on the Lord. The Chāndogya Upaniṣad (8.1.1) speaks of the small lotus in the heart, the city of Brahman, in which the whole universe is contained.',
 'Close your eyes and rest attention in the centre of the chest. Simple practices like this calm the whole being.')
DEEP[13]=('',
 'The Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad explains Oṃ as A-U-M plus the silence after: waking, dream, deep sleep and the fourth, pure consciousness. The Kaṭha Upaniṣad (1.2.16) calls Oṃ the imperishable Brahman itself. Patañjali (Yoga Sūtra 1.27–28) says the word expressing the Lord is <i>praṇava</i>, Oṃ, and its repetition with meaning leads to concentration. Commentators note that the Gītā joins sound and devotion: <i>vyāharan</i>, uttering, and <i>anusmaran</i>, remembering. Śaṅkara stresses Oṃ as a symbol of Brahman. Rāmānuja stresses remembrance of the Lord. The Gītā itself calls Kṛṣṇa “Oṃ in all the Vedas” (7.8).',
 'Try chanting Oṃ slowly three times, letting the sound fade into silence. Notice the calm that remains.')
DEEP[14]=('',
 'After the demanding yogic methods of 8.10–13, this verse gives an easier way. <i>Sulabha</i>, easily attained, is a treasured word in Śrī Vaiṣṇava tradition; Rāmānuja’s school lists <i>saulabhya</i>, accessibility, among the Lord’s chief qualities. Commentators note the conditions: <i>ananya-cetas</i>, undivided mind; <i>satatam</i>, always; <i>nityaśaḥ</i>, daily. Śaṅkara says for such a person the Lord is attained without the special techniques. Many devotees find this the most important verse of chapter 8: love, kept up daily, is enough.',
 'You don’t need complicated methods. Steady, loving remembrance every day is enough to keep the door open.')
DEEP[15]=('',
 '<i>Duḥkhālaya</i>, “abode of sorrow”, and <i>aśāśvata</i>, “impermanent”: the Gītā’s frank view of the world of rebirth. Commentators note it is not pessimism but realism: every birth brings ageing, illness, loss and death. Śaṅkara says the world is a place of sorrow because it is impermanent. Rāmānuja contrasts it with the Lord’s eternal abode. The Buddha’s teaching of <i>dukkha</i> is similar in spirit. The Gītā does not ask us to hate the world but to see it clearly, and to seek what lies beyond.',
 'See the world clearly, with its joys and sorrows. Clear seeing is the beginning of true freedom.')
DEEP[16]=('',
 'Puranic cosmology lists seven upper worlds, from Bhū (earth) through Bhuvar, Svar, Mahar, Janas and Tapas to Satya (Brahmaloka), Brahmā’s world. Commentators explain that even the highest of these is within time; at the end of Brahmā’s life, it too dissolves. Śaṅkara notes that those who reach Brahmaloka through meditation may gain liberation with Brahmā at the end of the cycle (<i>krama-mukti</i>, gradual liberation). Rāmānuja says only reaching the Lord himself ends rebirth. The verse puts every reward in perspective.',
 'Every reward within time ends. Let your deepest aim be something that does not.')
DEEP[17]=('',
 'In Purāṇic reckoning, the four yugas (Satya, Tretā, Dvāpara, Kali) together make a <i>mahā-yuga</i> of 4,320,000 human years. A thousand mahā-yugas make one day of Brahmā, a <i>kalpa</i>: 4.32 billion years. His night is as long. Brahmā lives a hundred such years. Commentators note the scale is meant to humble. Śaṅkara says knowing this cosmic time removes attachment to even the highest worlds. Some modern readers note that 4.32 billion years is close to the age of the Earth, a coincidence that intrigues many.',
 'Against cosmic time, most of our worries are very small. Let that perspective lighten today.')
DEEP[18]=('',
 'This is the Gītā’s picture of cosmic cycles: creation at Brahmā’s dawn, dissolution at his dusk. Commentators note the image of sleep and waking. As we wake each morning and the world reappears, and at night it vanishes, so the cosmos wakes and sleeps. Śaṅkara says the unmanifest is the seed-state of the world, held in the Lord’s power. Rāmānuja says it is subtle matter (<i>prakṛti</i>), which becomes gross at creation. The idea of cyclic time contrasts with linear creation stories in other traditions.',
 'Endings are part of a cycle. What dissolves will, in some form, begin again.')
DEEP[19]=('',
 '<i>Bhūtvā bhūtvā</i>, “having been, having been”: the repetition mirrors the endless cycle. <i>Avaśa</i>, helplessly, without control. Commentators note that the same beings return, carrying their karma, as a seed carries the plant. Śaṅkara says this helplessness is due to ignorance and karma. Rāmānuja says beings bound by karma are carried along until they reach the Lord. The verse contrasts sharply with the freedom of 8.21, where those who reach the supreme abode do not return.',
 'Much of life carries us along like a current. Freedom begins when we find what is not carried.')
DEEP[20]=('',
 'Two unmanifests are distinguished. The first is the cosmic seed (8.18) that holds the world between cycles. The second is beyond it, eternal, never dissolving. Commentators of every school agree this second is the Supreme. Śaṅkara calls it Brahman, the Self. Rāmānuja says it is the pure self, or the Lord. The Kaṭha Upaniṣad (1.3.11) has a similar ladder: beyond the great is the unmanifest, beyond the unmanifest is the Person, beyond the Person there is nothing.',
 'Behind everything that comes and goes, there is something that never goes. Look for it in quiet moments.')
DEEP[21]=('',
 '<i>Paramaṃ dhāma</i>, supreme abode, returns in 15.6. Commentators differ on what it is. Śaṅkara: the state of Brahman, not a place. Rāmānuja and the Vaiṣṇava schools: Vaikuṇṭha, the Lord’s eternal realm, where freed souls serve and enjoy him. Madhva: the Lord’s abode, where souls retain their individuality. All agree that it is beyond return. The verse completes the cosmology of 8.16–20 by naming the goal.',
 'There is a home beyond all our journeys. Knowing that can make the journey itself less anxious.')
DEEP[22]=('',
 '<i>Ananya bhakti</i>, undivided devotion, becomes central in chapters 9 and 11 (9.22, 11.54). Commentators note the shift: after cosmology, the means is simple. Śaṅkara says devotion here is knowledge in the form of love. Rāmānuja says it is loving meditation with no other object. The phrase “in whom all beings dwell, by whom all is pervaded” recalls 7.7 (pearls on a thread) and 9.4. The Lord is both container and pervader of all. Yet he is reached by something as simple as love.',
 'After all the big ideas, the way is simple: love with your whole heart.')
DEEP[23]=('',
 'Verses 23–26 describe two paths after death, drawn from the Upaniṣads. The Chāndogya (5.10) and Bṛhadāraṇyaka (6.2.15–16) describe the <i>deva-yāna</i>, the path of the gods, and the <i>pitṛ-yāna</i>, the path of the ancestors. Commentators note that “time” (<i>kāla</i>) here is read by Śaṅkara and Rāmānuja not as clock time but as presiding deities who guide the soul along each path. The teaching was well known in the Gītā’s day; Kṛṣṇa mentions it, then in 8.27 tells Arjuna not to worry about it.',
 'Old traditions map journeys after death in great detail. The Gītā’s practical advice comes in 8.27: live well, and don’t worry.')
DEEP[24]=('',
 '<i>Uttarāyaṇa</i> is the six months when the sun moves north, from the winter solstice to the summer solstice; it is celebrated at Makara Saṅkrānti. In the Mahābhārata, Bhīṣma, with the boon of choosing his death, lay on a bed of arrows for many days and waited for uttarāyaṇa to die. Commentators of the Vedānta schools read the stages as deities guiding the soul through realms of light. Śaṅkara says this path leads to Brahmaloka and gradual liberation. Rāmānuja says it leads to the Lord’s abode. Many Hindus still consider uttarāyaṇa an auspicious time to die.',
 'Live toward light: knowledge, kindness, truth. Then whatever path lies ahead will be bright.')
DEEP[25]=('',
 '<i>Dakṣiṇāyana</i> is the sun’s southward course, from summer to winter solstice. The <i>pitṛ-yāna</i>, the path of the ancestors, leads to the lunar realm, a heaven for those who did good works with desire for rewards. When their merit is used up, they return to earth (9.21). Commentators note that this is not a bad path; it is a path of reward within the cycle. Śaṅkara says “yogi” here means one who performed rites. Rāmānuja agrees. The smoke stands for unclarity, the moon for reflected light.',
 'Good done for reward brings reward, then returns. Good done for its own sake leads further.')
DEEP[26]=('',
 'Commentators simplify the two paths. The bright one is knowledge, devotion and selfless action, leading to freedom. The dark one is action done for reward, leading back to birth. Śaṅkara says the paths are eternal because the cycle of saṃsāra has no beginning. Rāmānuja says they correspond to the two kinds of practitioners. Many modern teachers read the verse ethically: every choice leans toward light or toward smoke. The two paths are not only after death; they are present in how we live now.',
 'Each choice today leans toward one path or the other. Choose light where you can.')
DEEP[27]=('',
 'This verse lightens the whole passage. Commentators say: having explained the two paths, Kṛṣṇa tells Arjuna not to worry about auspicious times. The yogi who lives in constant yoga is not confused about where to go. Śaṅkara says knowing the paths, the yogi chooses the bright one by living rightly. Rāmānuja says the devotee, remembering the Lord, is not anxious about the time of death. <i>Sarveṣu kāleṣu</i>, “at all times”, echoes 8.7: remember me at all times. The practical advice is simple: live well now.',
 'Don’t wait for an auspicious moment to start living well. Every moment is the right time.')
DEEP[28]=('',
 'Four sources of merit are named: study, sacrifice, austerity, charity. Commentators note that each is good and brings reward; the yogi goes beyond all of them. Śaṅkara says knowledge of the Self surpasses all merit. Rāmānuja says knowledge of the Lord’s nature, as taught in chapters 7 and 8, surpasses them. The verse is in triṣṭubh metre, giving the chapter a strong close. The colophon names chapter 8 Akṣara-Brahma Yoga. Chapter 9 will reveal the “royal secret” of devotion.',
 'Good works are good. Knowing and loving their Source is better still.')
